# Phase 2 Interview Transcript

**Participant:** Binoy Kanti Roy  
**Age:** 72 years old  
**Occupation:** Businessman

## Phase 2: Post-Deployment Evaluation Interview

### Section 1: The Affiliation Ledger and Negotiating Allegiance

**Interviewer:** Now that you have been using the agent for some time, how did it feel when the agent openly told you who it was working for?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes, yes, I noticed that. At first it felt a little different because normally these things just give a reminder. It does not tell you, I am working for you or I am working for your family. But actually I liked knowing it. I knew what it was doing.

**Interviewer:** Did that make the technology feel more transparent, or did it feel intrusive at home?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** More transparent. I don't like when something is doing something and I don't know. Here it tells me first. Sometimes if family is also involved, yes, that is okay. We already discuss medicine in our family. But I don't want every small thing to go to everybody.

**Interviewer:** Can you walk me through the last time the agent's role shifted from being private to involving your family?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** There was one time in the evening. There were people at home and we were talking, and I forgot the medicine time. The agent reminded me. I did not answer immediately. Then later it asked if it should involve my son. I allowed it. My son came and asked me whether I had taken the medicine. So that was how it happened.

**Interviewer:** When you allowed your son to become involved, did that feel like giving up your independence or more like an act of trust?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Trust, yes. I don't feel I am giving up my independence. I still take the medicine myself. My son is only helping me remember. If I am forgetting, why should I not take help from family. We already do this.

### Section 2: Veto Power, Risk, and Silence

**Interviewer:** When the agent wanted to alert your family about a missed dose, you had the option to stop it. Did you ever use that veto?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes, sometimes. If I knew I had taken the medicine and the agent did not know, then I stopped it. There is no reason to worry my sons for something that is not actually a problem.

**Interviewer:** What happened when the agent pushed back on your veto because the health risk was becoming higher?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** At first I was a little irritated. I thought, yes, yes, I heard you. Why are you saying again. But then I understood. If I keep saying no and actually I have missed the medicine, then that is not good. So if the system says the risk is getting higher, okay, then it can ask again.

**Interviewer:** Did that feel like it was taking away your control?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** No, not really. I still got the first chance. That is important. First tell me, then give me some time. If I don't do anything, then family can be told. That is reasonable.

**Interviewer:** What about the times when you were silent and did not respond at all?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Sometimes I was busy. Sometimes there were people around, sometimes I was outside. The agent reminded me again. That was okay. Silence does not always mean I forgot. Sometimes I am just doing something else.

**Interviewer:** Did you feel that the agent understood that, or did it sometimes treat your silence like a failure?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Mostly it understood. It gave me another chance. But if it keeps reminding again and again, then I feel, okay, I know, I know. It should give some time because our routine is not always exactly the same.

### Section 3: Care Collectivism and Checking as Love

**Interviewer:** Before the agent, your family sometimes checked whether you had taken your medicine. How has that changed now?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Earlier my sons would ask me, did you take the medicine, especially if I was busy or there was some family program. Now the agent reminds me first, so they don't have to ask every time. It has reduced that checking a little.

**Interviewer:** Do you miss those manual check-ins from your sons?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** No, no, I don't miss the repeated checking. But if my son asks me normally, that is different. I like that. Family should ask. The agent can remind me, but it cannot replace family.

**Interviewer:** Does the notification ever give your family a new reason to talk to you or show care?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes, sometimes. If the agent tells them I have not responded, my son will come and ask what happened. Then we talk. So in that way it gives them a reason to check. But I don't want them watching the phone all the time.

**Interviewer:** So would you say it has reduced care, or changed the way care happens?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Changed, yes. Not reduced. Before they remembered and asked themselves. Now the agent does one part, and then the family comes in if there is a real problem. The care is still there.

**Interviewer:** Think about a time when a prescription was unclear or you had to make a sudden health decision. Did the agent help in that family discussion or get in the way?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** There was one time when I was not sure about one medicine because the prescription was not very clear. I asked my son. We also asked the pharmacist. The agent was useful for reminding me what was already entered, but I did not take a new decision from the agent. For that, family and doctor or pharmacist should be involved.

**Interviewer:** So the agent can be part of the discussion, but not make the final decision?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes, exactly. It can tell us what is written or what the routine is. But if something changes, we have to discuss it.

### Section 4: Gamification as a Relational Trigger

**Interviewer:** When you reached a high score or a long streak, how did your family react?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** My sons noticed it. They were happy. One of them said, good, you are doing properly. I also felt good seeing that I had kept it for many days. It made the medicine routine a little more interesting.

**Interviewer:** Did your sons use your score as a reason to praise or encourage you?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes, yes. Sometimes they would ask what the score was. If it was good, they would say keep it like this. They were joking also, but it was encouragement.

**Interviewer:** Think back to a time when a streak was broken. Did losing the streak cause anxiety, or did your family encourage you?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** I was a little disappointed. I had kept the streak and then one day I forgot because there were people at home and I was busy. But my son said, okay, don't worry, start again. So I started again. It did not become a big problem.

**Interviewer:** Did sharing the score with your family make the experience better?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes, with family it is better. They can encourage me. But I don't want it to become like they are checking my score every day. It should be for encouragement, not pressure.

### Section 5: Probationary Trust and Retraining

**Interviewer:** When the agent was first introduced, did you put it on a probationary period before trusting it?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes, of course. I did not immediately trust it. First I wanted to see whether the reminders were coming at the right time. I already know my medicines, so I could compare.

**Interviewer:** How did you test whether it was trustworthy?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** I watched it for some days. I checked the medicine time myself and then saw what the agent said. If it matched, okay. If something did not match, I would ask my son or pharmacist. After some time, when it was giving the correct reminders, then I became more comfortable.

**Interviewer:** Did your long experience with taking medicines make it easier to judge the agent?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes, yes. I have been taking these medicines for a long time. So I know which medicine is for what and around what time I take it. The agent is helping my memory, not replacing everything.

**Interviewer:** Over the past few weeks, do you feel relying on the agent has replaced your own memory, or has it retrained your sense of when a dose is due?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** I think it has helped my memory. Earlier I remembered from habit. Now I still remember, but I also expect the reminder. Sometimes before the reminder comes, I already know it is nearly time. So it has not replaced my memory.

**Interviewer:** If the agent stopped working tomorrow, would you still know when to take your medicines?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Yes. I would manage. Maybe if I am travelling or there is some special program, then I could forget. But normally at home I know the routine. My family is also there.

### Section 6: Closing and the Plural Principal

**Interviewer:** If you had to summarize, who do you feel this AI agent ultimately works for, you, your family, or both?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** Both, both. It is for me because I have to take the medicine. But my family is also involved. If I forget, they want to know. So I think both.

**Interviewer:** Under what conditions is it acceptable for the agent to stop acting as your private tool and start acting as your family's advocate?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** If I miss the medicine and I don't respond after the reminder, then it can tell my son. If it is becoming a repeated problem, then definitely family should know. But one small delay, no need to involve everybody.

**Interviewer:** What would make that balance feel right for you?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** First remind me. Then give me some time. If I still don't respond, then tell my son. That way I can manage myself first, and if I cannot, family is there. That is how we normally do things anyway.

**Interviewer:** Any final thoughts about having the agent as part of your household?

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** It is useful. Especially when the routine changes, when I am travelling or there are people at home. At home I usually remember. But if the agent can remind me and then involve my family only when necessary, that is good. Keep it simple. No need to make it complicated.

**Interviewer:** Thank you very much.

**Binoy Kanti Roy:** You're welcome.
